objective:
After attending the module’s courses, the students will be able to:
- Carry out a thorough literature review related to political polarization;
- Understand the origin and the current state of political polarization in consolidated and hybrid democracies from the emergence of American democracy until today;
- Demonstrate the knowledge of how social media and the Internet influences political polarization;
- Differentiate between different types of political polarization, such as affective and perceived, elite and mass polarization;
- Differentiate between political polarization theories and evaluate their advantages and disadvantages.

After successful participation in this course, students are able:
to independently acquire knowledge and to critically discuss key aspects linked to sustainable and digital transformations, particularly in the context of smart cities;
to analyze how they can actively shape big transformations - to put their knowledge into practice for their own project, and to systematically plan and implement their own projects; to work together with an interdisciplinary team and to present their projects in a pitch

Upon successful completion, students are able to
– explain central theories from the psychology of learning and cognition and connect them to current research on AI-based learning systems;
– trace how empirical research questions are developed, investigated, and answered in our research group;
– understand and situate central methods of research on teaching and learning (including eye tracking and process and behavioral data) in their basic principles;
– critically read research findings and make them usable for the design of AI-supported learning environments;
– reflect on the opportunities, limitations, and ethical implications of AI-based systems in educational contexts.

In addition, students gain a realistic insight into academic work and into the thinking and approach of an empirically working research group.

